    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Libertarian arguments against taxation deeply stem from the principle of self-ownership, which posits that individuals have absolute moral rights over their own bodies and justly acquired property. From this perspective, involuntary taxation is equivalent to forced labor because it compels an individual to transfer the fruits of their justly earned effort to another's purposes. Furthermore, many libertarians appeal to Lockean precepts, maintaining that property rights are initially derived from labor, and any state interference outside of protecting those rights constitutes a violation of natural law.",
      "source": "target",
